# ts plot

`plot` draws the two plots that guide time-series modelling: the autocorrelation and partial autocorrelation functions with their white-noise confidence band, and the residuals against time. Each has two forms: a `*_payload` function returning the plotted numbers (for your own tables or plotting), and a drawing function that adds the picture to a `pluss::pl::Studio` figure, which can be saved as SVG, PDF or PNG (`save_svg(path)`, `save_pdf(path)`, `save_png(path, dpi)`).

## The Surface

| Entity | What it gives you |
|---|---|
| [`CorrPlotOptions`](plot/CorrPlotOptions.md) | Options of the autocorrelation plots and payloads |
| [`CorrPlotPayload`](plot/CorrPlotPayload.md) | The numbers behind an autocorrelation plot |
| [`plot_acf`](plot/plot_acf.md) | Draws the autocorrelation plot of a series into a `pluss::pl::Studio` figure |
| [`plot_acf_payload`](plot/plot_acf_payload.md) | The numbers behind the autocorrelation plot: lags, values and the white-noise band |
| [`plot_pacf`](plot/plot_pacf.md) | Draws the partial autocorrelation plot of a series into a `pluss::pl::Studio` figure |
| [`plot_pacf_payload`](plot/plot_pacf_payload.md) | The numbers behind the partial autocorrelation plot: lags, values and the white-noise band |
| [`plot_residuals`](plot/plot_residuals.md) | Draws the residuals of a model against time into a `pluss::pl::Studio` figure |
| [`plot_residuals_payload`](plot/plot_residuals_payload.md) | The numbers behind a residual plot: the time index and the residuals |
| [`ResidualPlotOptions`](plot/ResidualPlotOptions.md) | Options of the residual plot |
| [`ResidualPlotPayload`](plot/ResidualPlotPayload.md) | The numbers behind a residual plot |

## How To Call It

Compute the numbers behind the autocorrelation plot of an autoregressive series.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <cmath>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace ts = pluss::subsp::stats::ts;
using pluss::Series;

const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
void show(const char* label, const Series& s) {
  std::cout << std::left << std::setw(14) << label << std::right << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
  for (double v : s.host<double>()) std::cout << std::setw(9) << v;
  std::cout << "\n";
}
std::vector<double> noise(int n) {
  std::vector<double> e(n);
  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    double u = std::sin(12.9898 * (i + 1.0) + 78.233) * 43758.5453;
    u -= std::floor(u);
    e[i] = u - 0.5;
  }
  return e;
}
Series ar1(int n, double phi) {
  const auto e = noise(n);
  std::vector<double> x(n);
  double carry = 0.0;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    carry = phi * carry + e[i];
    x[i] = carry;
  }
  return pluss::make_series(x, {n}, f64);
}
Series walk(int n) {
  const auto e = noise(n);
  std::vector<double> x(n);
  double sum = 0.0;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    sum += e[i];
    x[i] = sum;
  }
  return pluss::make_series(x, {n}, f64);
}

int main() {
  const Series y = ar1(300, 0.7);
  ts::plot::CorrPlotOptions options;
  options.max_lag = 4;
  const auto payload = ts::plot::acf_payload(y, options);
  const auto lags = payload.lags.host<std::int64_t>();
  const auto values = payload.values.host<double>();
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < lags.size(); ++i) std::cout << "lag " << lags[i] << "  " << std::fixed << std::setprecision(3) << values[i] << "\n";
  std::cout << "95% band: +/- " << payload.conf_high << "\n";
}
```

